435 0 obj
<<
/Author (Firstname Lastname\054 Firstname Lastname and Firstname Lastname)
/CreationDate (D\07220220519180921\05308\04700\047)
/Creator (LaTeX with hyperref)
/Keywords (soft tactile sensor\073 flex sensor\073 multi\055directional force sensing\073 underwater soft robotics)
/ModDate (D\07220220519121453\05302\04700\047)
/Producer (pdfTeX\0551\05640\05621)
/Subject (Tactile information is crucial for recognizing physical interactions\054 manipulation of an object\054 and motion planning for a robotic gripper\073 however\054 concurrent tactile technologies have certain limitations over directional force sensing\056 In particular\054 they are expensive\054 difficult to fabricate\054 and mostly unsuitable for underwater use\056 Here\054 we present a facile and cost\055effective synthesis technique of a flexible multi\055directional force sensing system\054 which is also favorable to be utilized in underwater environments\056 We made use of four flex sensors within a silicone\055made hemispherical shell structure\056 Each sensor was placed 90 apart and aligned with the curve of the hemispherical shape\056 If the force is applied on the top of the hemisphere\054 all the flex sensors would bend uniformly and yield nearly identical readings\056 When force is applied from a different direction\054 a set of flex sensors would characterize distinctive output patterns to localize the point of contact as well as the direction and magnitude of the force\056 The deformation of the fabricated soft sensor due to applied force was simulated numerically and compared with the experimental results\056 The fabricated sensor was experimentally calibrated and tested for characterization including an underwater demonstration\056 This study would widen the scope of identification of multi\055directional force sensing\054 especially for underwater soft robotic applications\056)
/Title (Soft Multi\055Directional Force Sensor for Underwater Robotic Application)
>>
endobj
